original_source_statement_tex:
\label{theorem-descent}
The following conditions are equivalent.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] The morphism $f$ is a descent morphism for modules.
\item[(b)] The morphism $f$ is an effective descent morphism for modules.
\item[(c)] The morphism $f$ is universally injective.
\end{enumerate}

statement:
A homomorphism $f:R\to S$ of commutative rings is universally injective (or pure as an $R$-module map) if, for every $R$-module $T$, the canonical map $T\to T\otimes_R S$ is injective. Let $\operatorname{Desc}(S/R)$ be the category of $S$-modules $M$ equipped with an $S\otimes_RS$-linear isomorphism $\theta:M\otimes_RS\xrightarrow{\sim}S\otimes_RM$ satisfying the cocycle identity over $S\otimes_RS\otimes_RS$. The scalar-extension functor $f^*:\operatorname{Mod}_R\to\operatorname{Desc}(S/R)$ sends $T$ to $(S\otimes_RT,\mathrm{can})$. The following conditions are equivalent:
(a) $f^*$ is fully faithful (descent morphism for modules);
(b) $f^*$ is an equivalence of categories (effective descent morphism for modules);
(c) $f:R\to S$ is universally injective, equivalently pure as an $R$-module map.

